{-
   A **literal operand** of an operator whose name reaches more than one declaration.

   R5 pushes a position's type down only where a plain function is the whole overload set, so an
   untyped number standing in a set of several has nothing to take. Built at its default it then
   fits nothing: `lib/Core` writes `values(buffer) + at` and `48 + rest` against `Num.+` beside
   `lib/Native`'s pointer `+`, and an `I32` literal is neither a pointer nor the other operand's
   width - eight sites reported as "no + accepts these arguments".

   A literal is a `FromInt` call at a type to be decided, so what decides it is the position it
   fills. The operand is therefore left unbuilt while the candidates are tried: a candidate is
   viable when its *other* parameters fix the literal's position and a conversion instance exists
   there, exactly one viable candidate selects, and several is the ambiguity R2 already reports.

   The numbers are what say which candidate each operator reached and at which width the number was
   built - a rule that broke would change what this program computes rather than whether it compiles.
-}

data Tag {n: I64}

-- The plain half of the set at `(+, 2)` and at `(-, 2)`, beside Core's `Num.+` and `Num.-`. Neither
-- takes a number on the left, which is what makes the literal cases below decide on one candidate.
fn +(lhs: Tag, rhs: I64) -> I64 = lhs.n + rhs + 1000
fn -(lhs: Tag, rhs: I64) -> I64 = lhs.n - rhs - 1000

fn main() -> Int:
    let tag = Tag {n: 7}
    let wide = 40 :: I64
    let narrow = 3 :: U8

    -- A literal on the **left**, whose type the right operand decides. `Num.+` at `I64` is the one
    -- viable candidate: the plain `+` wants a `Tag` there and no number is one.
    if 2 + wide != 42 then return 1

    -- And on the **right**, which is the direction that already worked through the sole callee's
    -- push-down and has to keep working through a set.
    if wide - 2 != 38 then return 2

    -- The width is the *other operand's* and not `Int`'s. `narrow` is a `U8`, so the literal is a
    -- `U8` and the sum wraps at eight bits: 250 + 3 is 253, and 253 - 255 is 254.
    if 250 + narrow - 255 != (254 :: U8) then return 3

    -- The plain candidate still wins where its own position is what the operand fills, and its own
    -- body adds a thousand so that reaching it is visible in the answer.
    if tag + 5 != 1012 then return 4
    if tag - 5 != -998 then return 5

    -- A chain of nothing but literals has no other operand to read a width off, so each takes its
    -- own default - which is the same rule a literal in any other position obeys.
    if 1 - 1023 - 52 - 2 != -1076 then return 6

    return 77
